  "description": "Concatenate multiple pandas DataFrames vertically along axis 0 and reset the row index to form a consolidated table.\n\nVertical concatenation combines partitioned tabular batches (e.g., daily logs or sharded chunks) into a unified dataset.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\n\\text{df}_{\\text{stacked}} = \\operatorname{concat}([\\text{df}_1, \\text{df}_2], \\text{axis}=0, \\text{ignore\\_index}=\\text{True})\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Align columnar schemas across input DataFrames\n2. Concatenate tables vertically along row axis 0\n3. Reset integer row indices and return merged record list\n\n### Returns\nList of records representing the consolidated DataFrame.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Concatenate DataFrames\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nReal-world tabular datasets are frequently messy, containing missing values, duplicates, and unaligned columns. Pandas provides powerful DataFrame primitives for reshaping, filtering, and cleaning structured data before training machine learning models. **Concatenate DataFrames** performs structured data manipulation efficiently.\n\nVertical concatenation combines partitioned tabular batches (e.g., daily logs or sharded chunks) into a unified dataset. In this problem, the goal is to: concatenate multiple pandas dataframes vertically along axis 0 and reset the row index to form a consolidated table.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\n\\text{df}_{\\text{stacked}} = \\operatorname{concat}([\\text{df}_1, \\text{df}_2], \\text{axis}=0, \\text{ignore\\_index}=\\text{True})\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Align columnar schemas across input DataFrames\n2. **Phase 2**: Concatenate tables vertically along row axis 0\n3. **Phase 3**: Reset integer row indices and return merged record list\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Vectorized Array Operations**: Applying vectorized transformations operates on contiguous C memory buffers under the hood, running orders of magnitude faster than iterating with Python loops.\n* **Data Cleansing**: Handling duplicates and missing data prevents bias and data leakage into downstream machine learning models.\n* **Memory Management**: Choosing appropriate column dtypes (e.g. categorical or int32 instead of object) drastically reduces DataFrame memory usage.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: df1_records = [{\"id\": 1, \"val\": 10}, {\"id\": 2, \"val\": 20}], df2_records = [{\"id\": 3, \"val\": 30}, {\"id\": 4, \"val\": 40}]\n\nOutput: [{\"id\": 1, \"val\": 10}, {\"id\": 2, \"val\": 20}, {\"id\": 3, \"val\": 30}, {\"id\": 4, \"val\": 40}]\n\nExplanation: Evaluated pandas_concatenate_dataframes under standard test conditions."
